findings.body:
The diff renames the exported class from `SelectedCalendarRepository` to `PrismaSelectedCalendarRepository`, but nothing in the description asks for that, and two production routes still import the old name:

::code{file="apps/web/app/api/cron/calendar-subscriptions/route.ts" lines="11-11"}

::code{file="apps/web/app/api/cron/calendar-subscriptions/route.ts" lines="39-42"}

The webhook route has the same problem (`apps/web/app/api/webhooks/calendar-subscription/[provider]/route.ts`, lines 14 and 61). With the named export gone, `new SelectedCalendarRepository(prisma)` gets `undefined` at runtime, so the cron and webhook handlers throw before they do any work. TypeScript will also fail the web build.

The cleanest fix is to revert the rename. The class name adds nothing here, and reverting keeps the diff scoped to the feature. If the rename is intentional, update every importer in the same PR.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="5-5"}
export class SelectedCalendarRepository implements ISelectedCalendarRepository {

`SelectedCalendarRepository.test.ts` still imports `SelectedCalendarRepository` on line 3 and constructs it in `beforeEach` (line 53). After the rename that import is `undefined`, so `new SelectedCalendarRepository(mockPrismaClient)` throws in `beforeEach`. Every test in the file fails, including the new generic-suffix test. That contradicts the PR's claim that the listed test command validates the change.

::code{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.test.ts" lines="3-3"}

This finding goes away once the rename is reverted (see the critical finding). If the rename stays, update the import and the `new` call here as well.

`getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` is new public behavior on `DefaultAdapterFactory`, and it is the only place that decides which suffixes get filtered. `AdaptersFactory.test.ts` already covers `getProviders()` but has no test for the new method. The filtering tests in the repository and service tests use hardcoded suffix lists, so nothing checks that the factory returns the right list. A regression in the flatten or in the map keys would slip through.

::code{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="67-71"}

Add a test to `packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/__tests__/AdaptersFactory.test.ts` that asserts the four Google suffixes are returned and that the result contains no duplicates or empty strings.

`getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` flattens all providers into one list, and the repository applies that list to every integration in the batch. Today that is harmless, because Office 365 has no suffixes and Office 365 IDs are unlikely to end in Google's domains. But if someone later adds an Office 365 suffix, it would also drop Google rows. The reverse holds too. The suffix list is already keyed by provider, so the provider scope is available where the batch is built.

The fix is small. Either pass the providers into `getGenericCalendarSuffixes` and filter the map by them, or have the repository filter by `integration` and `endsWith` together. Either way, decide the scope on purpose rather than by accident.

::code{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="67-71"}

The three pre-existing `findNextSubscriptionBatch` tests gain `AND: undefined`. That line changes nothing: Vitest's `toHaveBeenCalledWith` ignores properties whose value is `undefined`. The assertion is therefore no stronger, and the churn adds noise. The only assertion that matters is the new one with real suffixes. A test for the no-suffix case that checks the `AND` key is absent, or that the query matches the old shape, would be more meaningful than these added lines.

::code{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.test.ts" lines="134-134"}

Remove the three added `AND: undefined,` lines. If you want to cover the no-suffix path explicitly, add one test that passes an empty array and asserts `findMany` was called without an `AND` clause.

The guard `genericCalendarSuffixes?.length` already proves the array is defined, so the second `?.` in the `.map` call is dead. The expression also reads more clearly if the array is built once.

